Boudewijn Waijers <kroisos@REMOVETHISWORD.home.nl> wrote:
>David Damerell wrote:
>>NetHack's all about resource management; and time can be made a
>>resource. Is it better to use turns now above Medusa, or explore
>>quickly in order to have time to retrieve loot from Gehennom? Magic
>>mapping becomes more than a convenience.
>Do you have a suggestion to impose such an incentive?

I think a number of interesting suggestions have been made. Rather than
applying a flat boost to monster difficulty, I would impose a minimum
depth in the average of depth and XL calculation based on the number of
turns passed. Let's say that your first 10,000 turns are free; thereafter
every 2,000 turns that minimum depth raises by one, so that for instance
on turn 20,000 you will always be considered to be at at least depth 5.

Also, the factor of 7 in 7*MONSTR for a monster to be considered too weak
to be generated could be reduced as time passed; perhaps by one every
20,000 turns starting on turn 40,000. That way the combination of rising
minimum depth and rising minimum monster difficulty would cause a gradual
shift towards nastier monsters; if you left it long enough, you would
practically ensure yourself a plentiful supply of Archons and other pain
in the endgame.

[Of course, there are many peculiarities in the current MONSTR scale which
could do with being looked at...]

Part of the puzzle, of course, is to remove the incentives for indefinite
scumming. Max HP ought to be capped; likewise divine protection,
intrinsic increase damage, all that kind of thing. It still ought to be
possible to get an impressively tooled-up character, but not to have
preposterous results like thousands of MaxHP and fifty points of increase
damage.

[While we're on MaxHP, the effect of player AC on monster damage is
phenomenal at high levels; in the late game, monsters who inflict physical
damage are a joke, with the sole exception of those air elementals on the
Plane of Air - and that's an artifact of the speed system (treating
maneuver and attack speeds separately wouldn't hurt, although I don't
propose to ruin speed boots as some Angband variants have). Presently the
player can accumulate so many HP and such a good AC that being surrounded
by eight minotaurs is not a problem. I think that's wrong; I think that
situation should demand some sort of emergency response. I don't want a
Crawl-style situation where all game you can be killed by about three
rounds of attacks from the wrong monster, mind; but you shouldn't be able
to stand there indefinitely going "la la la doesn't hurt a bit".]

Obviously the precise numbers here would need to be thought about more
carefully; I'm just pulling them out of thin air to give an example.

Monster difficulty could be informed in more subtle fashions. For example,
more spellcasters with high MONSTR could be added, distributed across
enough species that one couldn't simply genocide most of them out of the
picture. Monster spellcasting selection could then change as turns passed
to make the most unpleasant results more likely; if the chance of a
summoning storm rose as the game progressed, you'd have a definite
incentive to move along.
